WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.420
- I'm a journalist with a cause.

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:05.230
And I've been so active
against oppression,

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.170
against radical Islamism,
against the Muslim Brotherhood,

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.650
and against CAIR,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.120
which is the Council on American-Islamic--

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.362
- Relations.
- Relations.

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:17.195
- Yeah.

00:00:17.195 --> 00:00:21.040
- That are the, I call
them terrorists in suits

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.890
because these are the front
of the Muslim Brotherhood

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.220
and CAIR, who groomed Ilhan,

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.230
groomed Rashida and many others.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:33.330
And by the way, in 2020, we have more,

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.290
CAIR has been grooming more than 120

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.678
Rashida, Ilhan alike.

00:00:40.678 --> 00:00:43.261
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.480
- This is The Rubin
Report, and I'm Dave Rubin.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.290
Quick reminder, guys, if you
wanna see all our videos early

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.650
and ad free, join us at rubinreport.com.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.100
But more importantly, joining me today

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.550
is a first-generation
American, a journalist,

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.580
and now a candidate for Congress

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.520
in Minnesota's 5th District, you know,

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.230
the one currently
represented by Ilhan Omar.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.870
Dalia al-Aqidi, welcome
to The Rubin Report.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.850
- Thank you, Dave, thanks
for having me here.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.760
- I am very glad to have you here.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.610
There's gonna be a lotta stuff

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.590
that we're gonna unearth here today.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.630
But first let's do a little background.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.550
You are a first-generation
American from Iraq.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.770
You lived under Saddam, you
have a really interesting story.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:25.550
So I hand it to you.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.383
- Thank you.

00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:28.060
- Who is Dalia?

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.823
- Dalia is a journalist.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.010
I grew up in a very secular family,

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.513
I grew up in a family of arts.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:43.850
Both my parents are considered pioneers

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.490
in Iraqi modern theater, and my uncle.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.490
So I've been in, like, TV
business since I was a kid.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.010
And I grew up loving arts,

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.783
I grew up dancing ballet.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.720
And then in 1988,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.270
while, yes, people say I haven't suffered

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.913
personally from the regime,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.320
we had everything that we wanted,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.640
but we didn't have the freedom.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.773
And life without a freedom is,

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.882
it's not worth to live,
life without a freedom.

00:02:27.882 --> 00:02:28.820
- So what does that mean?

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.010
You're living a secular life,

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.360
your parents are secular and successful,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.320
but you're not free.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.023
What does that actually mean?

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.663
- We weren't, we didn't just fear Saddam.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.023
The way his regime was,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.510
it made you fear from your family members

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.430
or from your relatives,
or from your neighbors.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.310
You couldn't even joke or
mock Saddam or his family

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.650
or his government.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.060
You'll be dead.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.660
So you had everything,
but you can't speak,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.260
you can't give an opinion, you can't--

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.500
- So for people in the arts,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.300
this has gotta be doubly difficult.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.533
- Definitely, definitely.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.333
And this is, yes, my family didn't suffer,

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.430
but millions of Iraqis had suffered,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.500
and that's the question.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.360
Is it just about me or it's
about the whole country,

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.960
or it's about the whole people?

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.750
And that's when we decided to leave,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.750
my mother, my little, very
little brother, and myself.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.740
Even when we decided to leave the country,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.390
we couldn't speak at home.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.370
We would go, mom and
I, would go somewhere,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.680
you know, to a park and
park our car and walk

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.650
just to plan how are we
going to flee the country.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:04:00.500
None of my family knew
that we were leaving.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:01.840
- Meaning that if you had talked at home

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.040
you think your family members

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.866
would have ratted you out?
- Or it could be bugged.

00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:05.970
- Or bugged, wow.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:10.970
- Yes, because my mom
is a very famous actress

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.190
and you never know who's
listening to your phone calls,

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:20.110
who's listening to your
conversation even at home.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:20.943
- Wow.

00:04:20.943 --> 00:04:23.720
- So you're too scared to say
anything or to do anything.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.720
And we had friends that
their houses were bugged,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.810
and they disappeared, they were executed,

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:36.810
for sometimes saying a joke
or criticizing something.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.410
I mean, we couldn't even criticize

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.760
the wars that Saddam decided to wage.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.760
We couldn't criticize the
way he, the mass murder

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.060
that he did in Kurdistan,

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:54.820
which, I'm half Kurdish.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.010
We couldn't.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:00.080
So this is not the life that
anybody would want to live.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.080
It's suffocating when
you cannot do something

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:09.340
and when you want to make a
difference, but you can't.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.970
So that's how we decided to leave.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.613
And we just, each one
of us took two suitcases

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.910
and that's it, we left everything behind.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.220
- Yeah, I mean, did you
just hop on a flight

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:23.760
or what do you have to do to actually

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.253
get out of an oppressive regime?

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.600
- Thank you for asking this,

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.310
because in Iraq, you can't just--

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:34.830
- I may have to know in
case the progressives

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.519
take over in America, how you get out.

00:05:36.519 --> 00:05:37.641
- It's gonna, that's, yes.
- You know what I mean?

00:05:37.641 --> 00:05:38.570
So I'm doing this for--
- So you need--

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:39.660
- Self-preservation.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.220
- Yeah, so if they, which
they will never win,

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:49.220
but if the socialists win,
you, if you need to travel,

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.970
you have to get a permission to travel,

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.640
and they would write in your passport.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.380
Like, if I'm going to Athens, let's say,

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.940
they would write, she will go to Athens.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:01.810
I cannot go anywhere else.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.460
If I go somewhere else and come back,

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.840
I'll be in trouble, I'll be in jail.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:11.120
So what happened is, I was, back then,

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.600
because I studied stage
acting and directing,

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:19.600
so I was going to perform in
Tunisia in Carthage Festival.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.370
So I had the permission to leave.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:29.830
Mom had something, I think it was a movie

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.190
or something in Cairo,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.690
so they didn't notice that because we were

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:37.960
in different delegations,
that's what they call them,

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.750
so they didn't notice.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.750
So mom, because the
baby, she had a baby, so,

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.310
and she was supposed to
stay more than one month,

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.650
so she could take the baby with her.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:54.100
And we both left.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.973
I, after the festival,

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:04.830
I ran away from my team,
from the people I was with,

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:09.680
and I sent, I gave the Lebanese

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:14.898
delegation or, my two suitcases.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.560
So nobody knows where I'm going

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.617
because the head of
this delegation told me,

00:07:20.617 --> 00:07:25.617
"Dalia, somebody is going from
the embassy to stay here."

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.720
So I had to run away and it
was like, I was 20 back then.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.020
And for me, it was like
the longest journey.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.040
I didn't know because I left before mom,

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.463
so I didn't know if mom left or not.

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:46.720
I was too scared sitting,

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:50.470
I was flying to Athens,
and from Athens to Beirut

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.360
because that's where my aunt
is, that's where I'm supposed

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.610
to reunite with my mother and my brother.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:01.610
And until, because the embassies,

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.950
the Iraqi embassies during Saddam's era,

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.950
they were the centers of intelligence.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.660
So it's so easy for them to
grab you inside the embassy,

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:14.780
put you in a box and ship you back home.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.803
So anyways, to make the story short,

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.560
I got my luggage at the airport
from the Lebanese friends,

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.517
and then I sat down and I was like,

00:08:25.517 --> 00:08:29.370
"Please God, please God,
please God," until we took off.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.420
And when we took off, that was the freedom

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:35.360
for, my flight to freedom.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:36.370
- So that's when you flew.

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:38.900
So you flew from Lebanon
to America at that?

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:39.733
Oh, no, no, no, sorry.

00:08:39.733 --> 00:08:43.470
- No, I flew from Iraq to Tunisia.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:46.309
- Yeah, I got that one.
- And from Tunisia to Athens--

00:08:46.309 --> 00:08:47.142
- To Greece.

00:08:47.142 --> 00:08:48.050
- And then, yes.

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:49.813
And because I was Iraqi,

00:08:51.004 --> 00:08:53.260
I couldn't fly to the airport

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:58.260
because it was, back then, it
was controlled by the Syrians.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:03.000
So I had to stay eight hours in Greece

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:07.100
and then take another
flight to Larnaca, Cyprus,

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:11.483
and then take a 12-hour boat to Lebanon,

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.680
to the Christian area.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:18.060
And then, I mean, it
took me like three days,

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.677
alone, not knowing anything at
20 years old, but I made it.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.420
When I reached there,
nobody was waiting for me,

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.367
not my aunt, not, I had the address.

00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:29.470
"Okay, take me there."

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:34.100
I went there, it was during,
you know, the mess in Lebanon.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.000
So I reached the apartment,

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.030
it was on the 16th floor, no electricity.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:43.000
I left my suitcases after
three days of non-sleep.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.310
I took the stairs to the 16th floor,

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:50.080
knocked on the door,
mom, holding my brother,

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:51.050
opened the door.
- Wow.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:52.847
- I just looked, and I was like,

00:09:52.847 --> 00:09:57.430
"My suitcases are down,"
and I slept for two days.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:58.511
- Unbelievable.

00:09:58.511 --> 00:10:01.057
- It's like, it should
be a movie, by the way.

00:10:01.057 --> 00:10:02.300
- It should be a movie.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:05.970
Well, that really seems to
be only act one of the movie.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.880
Okay, so now you're in Lebanon.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:09.550
You've reunited with your mom.

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.050
Now what's the plan?

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:15.730
- And I got an offer from,

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:19.550
in UAE, from TV in Sharjah.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:24.550
So we went to Sharjah and that's when the,

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:27.563
Saddam invaded Kuwait.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.390
So I decided to do a show,

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:34.110
to show solidarity with the Kuwaitis

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:39.690
against Saddam, of course.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.732
- So this is like '91 or something?

00:10:41.732 --> 00:10:44.210
- No, 1990.
- '90, okay, yeah.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:48.710
- And then we were contacted
by the Iraqi opposition

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:52.643
to work together to topple Saddam Hussein.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:58.100
Our part was to open a radio station,

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:01.230
called Radio of the, of Free Iraq

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.360
and radio of the Iraqi opposition.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.253
So we went to Saudi Arabia,

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:09.950
and we were broadcasting from there.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:13.630
And that's when I met Chris
Stevens, the late ambassador.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.560
- Yeah, so can you
explain how Chris Stevens,

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:16.960
so the ambassador to Libya,

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.150
who obviously died in the Benghazi attack,

00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:20.110
can you explain a little bit

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:21.803
about your relationship with him?

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:24.710
- I've met him in Riyadh.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:28.253
I was in Jeddah, so
I've met him in Riyadh,

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.723
and we became friends.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:36.730
And whenever he comes to
Judah, we would get together,

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:40.530
and he's the one who made it happen.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:45.530
Because, at that time, we weren't like,

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:49.290
okay, we're living in Saudi Arabia,

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:54.290
we had a residency in
UAE because of my job.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:59.990
But because of our
background, my mom, myself,

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:05.520
and I had a death sentence
issued against me when I was 20.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:10.890
And he's the one who was
convinced and convinced me

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.400
that I should go to the US.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:18.320
People like us should be in the US.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.480
to have a brighter future.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.860
And he worked so hard
with the state department

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.388
to grant us political asylum.

00:12:26.388 --> 00:12:27.460
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:31.090
- And it's so sad that today

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:33.440
I live here and he's not there,

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:38.270
and I can never, never
forgive Hillary Clinton

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:43.270
for the last hour for
American patriots there.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:44.677
And at the end, she said,

00:12:44.677 --> 00:12:47.417
"At the end, what
difference does it make?"

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:53.133
I've watched her hearings
and it was so sad.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.150
- What else should we
know about Chris Stevens?

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:58.700
I mean, there were three others as well,

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.700
but he's the name that most people know.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:05.323
- He was such a beautiful human being.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:09.520
He learned Arabic,

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:13.440
and he has that self part that would...

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:20.030
He's very, not emotional,
but he understands, you know,

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.500
and he's the one who, when
we talk, he's the one.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:28.500
And since then, I've been twice to the US

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:31.140
as a tourist, because back then

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:32.310
when we were in Saudi Arabia,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.770
we had Saudi passports
because we were doing

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:39.770
this high-profile work

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:41.143
during that time.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:50.373
He just was one of the
best people I've ever met.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:54.480
He felt for the people.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.783
And that's, for the past
three years I've been,

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:02.500
I was doing a show from DC,

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.240
a weekly show for three years,

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:08.170
it's called "USL," "US Libya."

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:12.840
It's about US foreign
policy and US strategies

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.550
in North Africa in general,
in Libya in particular.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.270
And the people I've
worked with, the Libyans,

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.990
that they remember him.

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:27.990
And every Libyan I've met has
told me stories about him.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:32.450
And he was so committed to this country

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.740
and to the people, the Libyans people,

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:39.070
and he never, he wasn't scared

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.470
that this might kill him.

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:44.330
- Yeah, what is going on in Libya now?

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:45.900
Can you do, like, a quick recap?

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.461
Because it's sorta, we
did this military action,

00:14:48.461 --> 00:14:50.780
Benghazi, the whole thing, and it's like,

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:53.139
is even a functioning country anymore?

00:14:53.139 --> 00:14:56.020
- It's not so far, and the problem is

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:59.373
because the GNA, which is the NGA,

00:15:01.808 --> 00:15:05.360
the GNA, Government of National Accord,

